From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:33:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] gpl: restart Nesterov momentum where the HPWL rises near
 the end
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The momentum coefficient c_k = (a_k - 1) / a_(k+1) is about 1 - 3/(k+2):
0.994 by iteration 500. It is restarted after a non-virtual
timing-driven repair (GPL-0111), whose objective has changed, and on
divergence recovery, and nowhere else, while the density penalty under
it changes every iteration. Late in a long descent nothing damps the
motion: on a large design the fillers, which only the density moves,
speed up and take the cells with them, and the HPWL rises over the last
iterations of the placement.

XiangShan's XSTile parent, global_placement -skip_io (2.1 M movable
instances, target density 0.3, 2048 x 2048 bins), measured per
iteration with an instrumented build that has neither this change nor
the one before it, and places as the build without instrumentation
does: from iteration 540 to the stop at 554 the motion of all gcells
grows 31 times and the instances' 3.8 times, the fillers first, and the
step length 1.6 times; the HPWL rises 30 % at the iterate and 47 % at
the look-ahead, and the run ends at 98.5 M um. The first iteration
whose HPWL rose by more than the controller's reference is 545: 1.9 %,
with the density penalty controller at its floor (phiCoef 0.95) and the
overflow at 0.1575. Ways of restarting the momentum on the same
placement, each a switch in that build unless said otherwise:

- once, where the overflow first reaches 0.2 (iteration 521): the
  momentum regrows, 0 to 0.965 in 80 iterations, and the burst starts
  again before the target: 51.0 M um. The same once below 0.195
  (iteration 523), built as a change on top of the change before this
  one, not a switch: 58.2 M, the HPWL up 18 % in the last 13
  iterations.
- when the step points against the gradient (O'Donoghue and Candes'
  gradient restart): it never fires; the burst runs along the gradient.
  98.5 M, as without.
- on an iteration whose HPWL rose with the overflow at or below 0.2,
  the rule of this change: one restart, on iteration 545, then a calm
  descent to the target at 612, 48.2 M um; 47.86 M at 615 with a second
  switch that ends on the iterate, as the change before this one does,
  though without its window. The switch restarted before the
  convergence test and without the guards below.

This change itself, on top of the one before it, in bazel-orfs on the
same design's cached floorplan: -skip_io restarts once, on iteration
546 (overflow 0.1575, phiCoef 0.95), and ends at 47.86 M um at 615.
The timing- and routability-driven placement after it restarts once,
on iteration 516 (overflow 0.1347); the restart ends a routability
settle wait on the next iteration, pass 1 measures there, the phase
ends on pass 1's placement (GPL-0125), and the descent from it
converges at 844 with 32.3 M um, against 60.3 M without either change.
With no momentum step after a restart the cells draw together, 27 to
35 % of the HPWL within 5 to 9 iterations, and spread back to the
target with the HPWL nearly flat.

A regrowth that rises by less than the reference on every iteration is
not restarted. After the single restart at iteration 521 above, the
HPWL at the iterate rose 4.5 % from iteration 588 to 603 with phiCoef
between 1.0005 and 1.04: this change, run alongside it, would not have
fired once. With this change's rule as the switch, the descent after
its one restart came down to the target without another rise by the
reference, and ended 0.5 % above the lowest of its table rows (one
every 10 iterations) after the restart.

On a synthetic design of 1 M cells (utilization 0.145, target density
0.3, 67 % fixed macros, 2048 x 2048 bins) the cells run away from
overflow 0.11 (HPWL 134.3 M um at iteration 888, 164.8 M at 909, the
overflow stalling); the same rule as a switch, with the level at 0.3,
restarts once, after iteration 895, and the run converges at 896 with
134.5 M.

NesterovPlace::restartMomentumOnRise() restarts the momentum (curA = 1,
as the repair's restart does, so the next iteration takes no momentum
step) on any iteration whose unscaled overflow is at or below 0.2 and
whose density penalty controller has measured a rise: phiCoef below 1,
which it is when the HPWL rose by more than referenceHpwl since the last
iteration. It logs GPL-0126 with the iteration, the overflow and
phiCoef. There is no once-per-descent flag: a descent that builds its
momentum up again and rises by the reference again restarts again.
Every restart is on an iteration whose HPWL rose by more than the
reference; it adds no iteration and reverts nothing, so the iteration
limit bounds the loop as before. Consecutive restarts are possible,
when an iteration with no momentum step still rises, and keep the
descent a plain gradient descent while they last, with the controller
lowering the penalty in each.

0.2 is where checkDivergence() starts to read a rising HPWL as a
divergence (GPL-0323). It is below the routability check overflow (0.3
by default): a descent between routability passes gets there only while
a pass waits for its cells to settle (GPL-0099), and a restart there
usually ends the wait on the next iteration (GPL-0103), as a non-virtual
repair's restart already does. Not the lowest timing-driven overflow:
the repairs are not what makes the motion run away, and a list of
overflows a user sets should not move it.

What else owns an iteration:
- A non-virtual timing-driven repair on it restarts the momentum itself;
  that restart (GPL-0111) is the only one and the only log line.
- A routability pass on it may revert the placement whose HPWL was
  measured (GPL-0087, -0089, -0125) or change its cells' sizes; no
  restart, the next iteration measures its own rise.
- A divergence revert (GPL-0112, -0998) skips the rest of the iteration
  and restarts the momentum itself.
- The restart comes after the convergence test: an iteration on which
  the placement converges, on its iterate or not, never restarts.
- Several regions: each region's phiCoef reads the HPWL of the whole
  design; the lowest phiCoef of the regions still descending counts. A
  region that has converged no longer moves, and its phiCoef does not
  count.
- Incremental placement: nothing is kept between doNesterovPlace()
  calls.
- GPU: curA is the host's and phiCoef is measured on the host on both
  paths, as for the repair's restart; the GPU tests were not run.

Of gpl's tests (84 logs), 82 are identical to the change before this
one alone, the large tests included, and the other two differ by their
new cases: none of the 116 placements in them restarts. That follows from
their size, and says nothing about a larger design: the reference is
the larger of 446,000,000 DBU and 1 % of the initial HPWL, 4.4 % of
large01's final HPWL (5.1 M um at 2,000 DBU per um) in one iteration,
so no test design rises by it near its end. On XSTile it is 0.9 % of
the final HPWL. The larger the design, the smaller the rise, as a
fraction of its HPWL, that restarts, until 1 % of the initial HPWL
takes over from the floor.

Tests:
- end_of_descent_test MomentumRestartsWhereTheHpwlRises: gcd from the
  core's center with a reference of 5,000 DBU, so that its rises
  register; 21 restarts, three of them on consecutive iterations,
  exactly on the iterations at or below 0.2 whose penalty fell; a twin
  stopped on the first restart has 316 look-aheads off their iterates,
  one stopped on the next iteration none. The run converges.
- end_of_descent_test MomentumRunsOnWithoutARise: gcd with the default
  reference, below 0.2 from iteration 316 to 355 with no rise: no
  GPL-0126, and the look-aheads are still apart (315) on the iteration
  after 316 and on the last.
- end_of_descent_test MomentumRestartsOnlyOnARiseNearTheEnd: the
  decision for overflows 0.5 to 0.05 and phiCoef 0.95 to 1.04, and none
  with a repair restarting, a routability pass, or only a converged
  region's phiCoef.
- routability_minimum_test MomentumRestartStandsAsideOnAPassIteration:
  a routability-driven descent of gcd with its passes at overflow 0.2,
  RUDY reading congestion and the 5,000 DBU reference: six passes, each
  reverting, five of them on an iteration whose HPWL rose; no restart
  on a pass's iteration, 17 after the phase, converged at 9,076.
Without the restart the first, the third and the fourth fail (the
fourth: no restart in the descent); restarting at every iteration at or
below 0.2 fails the first three, and the change before this one's two;
once per descent, the first and the third; with the restart logged but
curA not reset, the first and the third; without the overflow level,
the first and the third; without the repair guard or with a converged
region's phiCoef read, the third; without the routability pass guard,
the third and the fourth (a restart on six passes' iterations); with
the call site never reporting a pass, the fourth. The repair guard is
tested on the decision only: no test design rises near its end with a
non-virtual repair.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

